<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.11.3: Finding a Quadratic Function from Its Zeros and a Point</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02276</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.11.3: Finding a Quadratic Function from Its Zeros and a Point</md:title>
    <md:uuid>ee68937f-dc7c-4e43-b67c-f1e51d01ea2e</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>When finding an exact function that has particular zeros and passes through a certain point, we use the intercept form of a quadratic function:</para>
<para>\(f(x) = a(x − p)(x − q)\)</para>
<para>The variables \(p\) and \(q\) represent the zeros or roots of the function. The variable \(a\) accounts for any constant that might have been factored out when solving.</para>
<para>Remember, if needed in the following problems, use the "^" symbol to enter exponents.</para>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Find a function, \(m(x)\), in intercept form, that has the zeros –5 and 8.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter a function, \(m(x)\), in intercept form.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>The intercept form is \(m(x) = a(x − (-5))(x − 8)\). </para>
    <para>\(= a(x + 5)(x − 8)\) </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<para>Let's use the intercept form of the function above to find the exact function that passes through \((5, -60)\).</para>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>First, find the value of \(a\). To do this, substitute the values of the point into the intercept form of the function.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the value of \(a\).</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(m(x) = a(x + 5)(x − 8)\) 
      \(-60 = a((5) + 5)((5) − 8)\) 
      \(-60 = a(10)(-3)\) 
      \(-60 = -30a\)
        \(2 = a\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Substitute the value of \(a\) into the intercept form of the function and multiply to find the standard form.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the standard form of the function.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> \(m(x) = a(x + 5)(x − 8)\)
      \(m(x) = 2(x + 5)(x − 8)\)
      \(m(x) = 2(x^2 + 5x − 8x − 40)\)
      \(m(x) = 2(x^2 − 3x − 40)\)
      \(m(x) = 2x^2 − 6x − 80\)</para>
    <para>This is the function with zeros –5 and 8 that passes through \((5, -60)\).</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Write the quadratic function, \(n(x)\), in standard form, that has the zeros 9 and –2 and passes through the point \((0, -36)\).</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the quadratic function, \(n(x)\), in standard form.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(n(x) = 2x^2 − 14x − 36\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Write the quadratic function, \(p(x)\), in standard form, that has the zeros –6 and –4 and passes through the point \((-5, 3)\).</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the quadratic function, \(p(x)\), in standard form.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(p(x) = −3x^2 − 30x − 72\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Write the quadratic function, \(c(x)\), in standard form, that has the zeros 1 and 7 and passes through the point \((9, 16)\).</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the quadratic function, \(c(x)\), in standard form.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(c(x) = x^2 − 8x + 7\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

